<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Jolof</h1><p class="st-meta">1549 CE – 1890 CE · capital Yang-Yang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Wolof heartland kingdom left after the break-up of the Jolof Empire, whose last king Alboury Ndiaye resisted French conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Until the mid-16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buurba-jolof">Buurba Jolof</a> ruled an empire over all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> states. In 1549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amari-ngone-sobel-fall">Amari Ngoone Sobel Fall</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a> defeated and killed the Buurba at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danki">Danki</a>, and the coastal provinces broke away. What remained was an inland kingdom centred on Yang-Yang, cut off from direct trade with European ships.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Buurba was chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> from royal lineages and ruled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> farmers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders of the dry interior. Without access to the coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> was poorer than <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/waalo">Waalo</a> and was often drawn into their wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread steadily during the 18th and 19th centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboury-ndiaye">Alboury Ndiaye</a>, who became Buurba in 1875, built alliances against the French but was forced out in 1890 and fled east, dying in exile around 1901. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>, and its name is still used for the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linguere">Linguere</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-jolof.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Jolof" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Jolof_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1549 CE – 1890 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yang-Yang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alboury Ndiaye</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jolof, Djolof</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1720&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jolof" aria-label="Kingdom of Jolof on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4267 1605 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Jolof lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4587 1861L4587 1861M4587 1861L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4587 1861h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4587 1861h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4587 1861h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1720&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jolof">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
